WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:06.090
The star-studded House of Gucci is inspired
by the true story of Italy's infamous Black

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:11.379
Widow who conspired to murder her ex-husband,
but how much of the film is Hollywood fiction

00:00:11.379 --> 00:00:13.240
versus historical fact?

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:17.430
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan, and
in this video I'm revealing the biggest things

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:23.040
that House of Gucci got wrong about the real
story of Patrizia Reggiani and the Gucci family.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.420
The true tale of Patrizia Reggiani who came
from humble beginnings and married into the

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:32.840
rich and powerful Guccis has plenty of intrigue
and drama, however, House of Gucci isn't shy

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:36.839
about spicing things up, including Patrizia's
wedding dress.

00:00:36.839 --> 00:00:39.879
The movie's costume designer prepared two
designs.

00:00:39.879 --> 00:00:44.190
The first was modelled on the real dress worn
by Patrizia which covered her all the way

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:45.449
down to the floor.

00:00:45.449 --> 00:00:50.710
But director Ridley Scott opted for an alternate
design that was more revealing and is a homage

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:54.149
to famous Italian actress Gina Lollobrigida.

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:57.390
According to the film's costume cutter,
"even though the [wedding] scene was short,

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:02.870
it was significant for the character, so the
outfit had to have immediacy and impact."

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:06.931
In the movie, Maurizio meets his childhood
friend Paola when he flees to St Moritz to

00:01:06.931 --> 00:01:09.490
get away from the Italian tax authorities.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:14.320
The film plays up the idea that his romance
with Paola began while he was still with Patrizia,

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.659
and also that he decided to separate from
his wife while they were together in Switzerland.

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:23.480
In fact though, the details of Maurizio's
split with Patrizia were much different.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:28.759
It happened one weekend in 1985 when he told
Patrizia he was going on a business trip to

00:01:28.759 --> 00:01:31.540
Florence, left with a suitcase and never came
back.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:35.540
It was left to a friend to tell Patrizia the
next day that it was actually over.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:41.729
Also, in real life, Maurizio didn't reunite
with Paola until 1990, a full five years after

00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:43.290
he left his wife.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:47.229
On top of that, the movie completely omits
the fact that Maurizio was actually having

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:52.240
an affair with a different woman, Sheree McLaughlin
who said their relationship spurred him on

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.880
to leave his unhappy marriage.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:59.210
Maurizio's affair with McLaughlin lasted until
he met up with Paola years later which did

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:03.570
happen in Switzerland though it was actually
at a club and not on the ski slopes.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:09.030
I suspect the filmmakers omitted the relationship
with McLaughlin to condense the story given

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:13.769
the final film is already over two and a half
hours long and they chose to portray Paola

00:02:13.769 --> 00:02:17.320
over her as that's who he's with at the time
of his death.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:22.840
Also, having Patrizia share scenes with Paola
ramps up the movie's drama before the separation.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:24.900
You ever steal anything Paola?

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:29.430
I'm trying to teach Alessandra the importance
of not touching other people's belongings.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:33.182
The details surrounding how Patrizia would
eventually have Maurizio killed have also

00:02:33.182 --> 00:02:35.709
been compressed and dramatized for the movie.

00:02:35.709 --> 00:02:39.790
In reality, it appears she didn't meet with
the people involved in the murder as happens

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:42.920
in the film;
instead, she asked lots of different people

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.519
if they'd help her, then she got her psychic
friend Pina to act as an intermediary and

00:02:47.519 --> 00:02:48.519
find the assassins.

00:02:48.519 --> 00:02:52.060
There was also another person involved who
was the real-life getaway driver;

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:56.560
however, in the film his role has been merged
with one of the other conspirators.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:01.069
Also in the movie, the assassin shoots Maurizio
three times, twice in the back and once in

00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:03.670
the chest;
but according to the real-life concierge who

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:08.760
witnessed the murder, the gunman shot Maurizio
a total of four times, three in the back and

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.709
a fourth time in his head.

00:03:10.709 --> 00:03:13.920
This may have been changed to make it a little
less gory in the movie.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.689
And there's a touch of extra drama in the
trial with Patrizia refusing to answer to

00:03:18.689 --> 00:03:22.530
her maiden name Reggiani,
which in reality she also did when signing

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:27.439
her police statement, defiantly telling the
judge to call her Signora Gucci, a reference

00:03:27.439 --> 00:03:32.540
to how she continued to use the Gucci name
even after her divorce and how she's popularly

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:34.520
known as Lady Gucci.

