00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:52.310
The filmmakers also took some creative license
when staging Maurizio's murder.

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:55.360
The movie's production designer has said that
the spot in Milan on the Via Palestro where

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:00.400
the real murder took place "was drab and uncinematic
and [they] were striving for a more heightened

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:01.400
atmosphere."

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:05.620
So he says they found "a section of Rome with
wide streets that could credibly stand in

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:06.620
for Milan.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:10.840
And the area was a collage of architectural
styles, Gothic, Renaissance and Arabic [that

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.759
gave it] a very operatic feel."

00:05:13.759 --> 00:05:18.500
The day that Maurizio was killed, Patrizia
famously wrote in her diary the single word

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:21.960
"paradeisos", which in Greek means "paradise".

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:26.090
In the movie, however, she writes the Italian
version of the word, "paradiso".

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:30.919
I imagine the filmmakers did this as they
thought it seemed more logical and more people

00:05:30.919 --> 00:05:35.240
would easily and quickly understand the Italian
word than the Greek one.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:40.850
The film team also opted for a much more dramatic
way to show how Patrizia had Paola evicted

00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:43.190
from the home she'd been sharing with Maurizio.

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:49.010
In the movie, Patrizia turns up at the apartment,
and after embracing Paola tearfully, orders

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:51.870
her to be shown out of what she now calls
her house.

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:55.000
I subscribe to unconventional punishment.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.139
Back in the real world, this never happened.

00:05:58.139 --> 00:06:03.349
Instead, the eviction order was delivered
via a notarised letter drawn up around 11am

00:06:03.349 --> 00:06:07.699
on the same day Maurizio died, just a few
hours after his death.

00:06:07.699 --> 00:06:12.449
Although Patrizia and Maurizio's daughter
Alessandra appears in the film, their second

00:06:12.449 --> 00:06:15.490
child Allegra was completely cut from the
story.

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:20.530
I suspect this was done to condense the already-long
movie as even the daughter who does appear

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:23.060
isn't a crucial part of the script.

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:28.090
Also it means the filmmakers could avoid casting
more child actors whose involvement in a production

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:33.200
brings additional rules and regulations, especially
as they'd already had to get three actors

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:36.430
to play Alessandra over a range of ages.

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:41.190
Another interesting detail absent from the
final film is that after Patrizia was convicted,

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:45.930
her daughters tried to get a retrial on the
basis that after their mother had an operation

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:51.379
to remove a brain tumour in 1992, she lacked
proper control over her mental faculties at

00:06:51.379 --> 00:06:52.840
the time of the murder.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:55.810
This did get her sentence reduced by a few
years;

00:06:55.810 --> 00:07:00.620
however, since then both daughters seem to
have more or less cut ties with their mother.

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:04.349
When it comes to the look of the characters,
although the movie's make-up and hair team

00:07:04.349 --> 00:07:08.990
seem to have done a good job with Patrizia,
the real Gucci family have complained about

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:12.550
some of the cast, claiming their depictions
are unflattering.

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:17.300
One of Maurizio Gucci's cousins has said her
grandfather Aldo was a lot taller than Al

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:23.099
Pacino who plays him and that Pacino's overall
heavier look and sideburns compared unfavourably

00:07:23.099 --> 00:07:26.789
to the real-life Aldo who she says was much
more handsome.

00:07:26.789 --> 00:07:32.110
Plus, she also felt offended by Jared Leto's
untidy, unfashionable look as Paolo.

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:34.060
Paolo, you are a Gucci.

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:37.000
You need to dress the part.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:38.110
It's chic.

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:42.460
After the release of the first trailer, some
suggested Lady Gaga's accent was more Eastern

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:44.419
European than Italian.

00:07:44.419 --> 00:07:49.030
And since then, Francesca De Martini – an
Italian actress who worked as a dialect coach

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:53.720
for Salma Hayek who plays Pina – commented
that while she was listening to Hayek's scenes,

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:58.030
she thought Gaga's "accent was a bit off",
sounding "more Russian" than Italian.

00:07:58.030 --> 00:08:02.449
I don't consider myself to be a particularly
ethical person..

00:08:02.449 --> 00:08:03.729
but I am fair.

00:08:03.729 --> 00:08:08.840
De Martini also said there were a lot of different
accents in the film with, in her opinion,

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:11.270
Al Pacino sounding "more New York Italian".

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:14.300
Gucci...
is what I say it is.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:16.790
And Adam Driver's accent being "nearly non-existent".

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:19.450
As far as fakes go, they're pretty good.

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:22.370
At least it's my name on the mugs, not yours.

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:26.759
And then there's Jared Leto's Paolo which
you can make your own mind up about.

00:08:26.759 --> 00:08:28.050
These are just mock-ups.

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:29.160
I can't afford to get serious.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:31.849
You picked a real firecracker.

00:08:31.849 --> 00:08:36.200
According to De Martino, there wasn't a dialect
coach on set and she was only brought in at

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:40.550
Hayek's request after the actress felt she
wasn't getting the accent quite right.

00:08:40.550 --> 00:08:43.341
Don't let the forces around you dictate who
you are.

00:08:43.341 --> 00:08:46.090
You are strong, you are magnificent.

00:08:46.090 --> 00:08:51.020
Now, in Lady Gaga's defence, she revealed
in an interview with Vogue that her method

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:54.090
to get into character as Patrizia was very
intense.

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:57.850
She said she "lived as her for a year and
a half [and] spoke with an accent for nine

00:08:57.850 --> 00:09:01.330
months of that", apparently never breaking,
even off camera.

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:03.410
If you don't know, I'm gonna make sure you
do.

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:07.400
She also said "it was impossible for [her]
to speak in the accent as a blonde," so she

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:09.440
instantly died her hair.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:13.650
And she added that she worked hard on figuring
out Patrizia's specific accent.

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:14.650
Bravo.

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:18.240
And uses different ones depending on who she's
talking to in the film.

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:19.770
It's time to take out the trash.

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:24.250
Gaga began with a dialect from Vignola, where
Patrizia was born, and then moved on to what

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:29.020
she described as a "higher class way of speaking
that would have been more appropriate in places

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:30.840
like Milan and Florence".

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:35.000
Whatever you think of the accents though,
all credit [goes] to Lady Gaga for improvising

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:37.690
a line that's already become iconic.

00:09:37.690 --> 00:09:39.610
Can you keep a secret?

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:42.130
Father, Son and House of Gucci.

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:46.760
So, what do you think of House of Gucci and
who gave the best and worst performances?

